I see what you’re trying to do here. You’re essentially counting all the `0`s, then all the `1`s, then all the `2`s, and pushing them in order into a new vector `total`. That works as a brute-force approach, but there are **two main problems** in your code:  

1. **You never assign the result back** to `nums`. Right now, you’re only filling `total`, but `nums` remains unchanged.  
2. **Unused variable `m`** — it’s declared but never used.  

Here’s the **corrected version of your brute force code**:  

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    void sortColors(vector<int>& nums) {
        vector<int> total;

        // Push all 0s
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.size(); i++) {
            if (nums[i] == 0) {
                total.push_back(0);
            }
        }
        // Push all 1s
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.size(); i++) {
            if (nums[i] == 1) {
                total.push_back(1);
            }
        }
        // Push all 2s
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.size(); i++) {
            if (nums[i] == 2) {
                total.push_back(2);
            }
        }

        // Copy result back to nums
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.size(); i++) {
            nums[i] = total[i];
        }
    }
};
```

✅ This will now work correctly. It’s **O(n)** in time, but uses **extra O(n) space**.  

