Good question 👌  

To be blunt: **No, there isn’t a strictly “better” way than Dutch National Flag** for this problem.  
Here’s why:  

### Options you have  

1. **Brute force with extra array (your first approach)**  
   - Time: O(n)  
   - Space: O(n) → not optimal, uses extra memory.  

2. **Counting sort style (count 0s, 1s, 2s, then overwrite nums)**  
   - Time: O(n)  
   - Space: O(1)  
   - Simple to implement, but requires **two passes** (one to count, one to overwrite).  

   Example:  
   ```cpp
   class Solution {
   public:
       void sortColors(vector<int>& nums) {
           int c0 = 0, c1 = 0, c2 = 0;
           for (int x : nums) {
               if (x == 0) c0++;
               else if (x == 1) c1++;
               else c2++;
           }
           int i = 0;
           while (c0--) nums[i++] = 0;
           while (c1--) nums[i++] = 1;
           while (c2--) nums[i++] = 2;
       }
   };
   ```
   ✅ Very clean and readable.  
   ❌ But not strictly one-pass.  

3. **Dutch National Flag (optimal)**  
   - Time: O(n)  
   - Space: O(1)  
   - One pass, in-place, no counting.  
   - This is the standard interview-expected solution.  

---

🔑 **Takeaway:**  
- If you want **simplicity → use counting sort**.  
- If you want **the cleanest optimal solution → Dutch National Flag** (this is the best balance between efficiency and in-place requirement).  

